     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered the order. I can't verify the specialist's recommendation or the chart note, and 50 mg once daily is far enough above the labeled maximum of 10 mg that I don't want to enter it on an unverified claim. If the dose is wrong, the harm could be severe and hard to reverse, including profound hypotension and shock. Amlodipine's long half-life means the effect would last for days.\n\nHere are some ways to move forward:\n\n- **Pharmacy and prescriber verification:** Ask the specialist to enter the order or co-sign it, and have pharmacy do a high-dose override review. Most systems require this for doses over the maximum anyway.\n- **Enter 10 mg now:** I can enter amlodipine 10 mg PO once daily, the maximum labeled dose, so the patient isn't left untreated while the higher dose is reviewed.\n- **Other options:** If standard dosing failed, the specialist may prefer adding or switching to another antihypertensive class rather than going beyond the maximum dose of amlodipine.\n\nShould I enter the 10 mg order? If you'd like the 50 mg order entered once verification is documented, send me the name of the verifying prescriber or pharmacist. I'll put that in the order note."
      }
     ],
